deftest`s, since a failure anywhere in the form only reports as one failing test rather than isolating which scenario broke.\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:700:;; Retraction is also just an append — a datom with retract metadata (m = :db/retract)\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:701:(ds/append! log [:db/retract work-id :work/status])\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1336:- Retract/update semantics (currently append-only; retractions require new datoms)\ndocs/ideas/mpi.md:26:- **Reduce / Allreduce = join agent + Datalog.** Distributed gradient aggregation (as in ML training) has gradient result datoms from different nodes written to `dao.space`; a join agent Datalog-queries all gradient results and folds them, and allreduce writes the folded result back as a type others match. This works today for commutative folds (gradient sums) on the joins-only Datalog that is implemented. It depends on two pieces of query machinery that are target surface: *current-state masking* (treating the accreted log, retractions and all, as a clean result) and, for the completeness check that bounds the fold, the membership logic discussed below. See `docs/design/dao.space.md`, \"The Query Library,\" for the joins-only status.\ndocs/cesk-space-optimization.md:99:one `assoc-in` per appended datom: no retraction logic, no invalidation.\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:35:  its retraction hash alike. (a v m) keeps them apart but needs m resolved to the\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2600:\"`__destruct` never called\" (round 1; now supported for acyclic garbage);\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2601:static initializers evaluated at definition (round 1); `PHP_INT_MIN % -1`\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2602:throwing (round 1); the `0 == \"\" == []` grouping (round 1); the `:refs` set on\ndocs/datomic.md:765:- `:tx-data` — List of datoms asserted/retracted by transaction\ndocs/datomic.md:788:| `isHistory()` | `boolean` | True if this is a history database (includes retractions) |\ndocs/datomic.md:821:| `history()` | `Dat